---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RackViewer from '../components/RackViewer.astro';
import S from '../components/S.astro';
import T from '../components/T.astro';
import L from '../components/L.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Deeper from '../components/Deeper.astro';
import SpecTable from '../components/SpecTable.astro';
import { partInfo } from '../scripts/rack-parts';
---
<Base
  title="Anatomy of the Rack"
  description="Eighteen compute trays, nine NVSwitch trays, a copper NVLink spine, a busbar and a coolant loop — the GB200 NVL72 pulled apart part by part."
  kicker="Chapter 02"
  heading="Anatomy of the Rack"
  slug="/anatomy"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      Twenty-seven trays on a regular pitch, a spine of copper at the back, a bar of copper down
      one side and water through all of it. Drag the model, pull the explode slider, and click
      anything that looks interesting.
    </p>
  </div>

  <RackViewer
    caption="Schematic model, generated from the dimensions in the table below rather than from CAD. Tray pitch and group ordering follow public teardown photography and OCP mechanical documentation; it is an explanatory diagram, not a fabrication drawing."
  />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="stack">Reading the stack</h2>

    <L at={1}>
      <p>
        Look at the model from the front and it is a stack of thin drawers. Most of them —
        eighteen — are <strong>compute trays</strong>, and those hold the chips that do the actual
        work. Nine more, sitting in the middle of the stack, are <strong>switch trays</strong>:
        they do no computing at all, they just connect the other trays to each other.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rest of the rack exists to keep those twenty-seven drawers fed and cool. There is a
        bar carrying electricity down the side, pipes carrying water up the back, and a thick
        bundle of cables joining every compute tray to every switch tray.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        The <S id="compute-trays" /> compute trays and <S id="switch-trays" /> switch trays are
        both 1U, on the same pitch, in the same rails. The ordering is what carries information:
        the switch trays sit in the <em>middle</em> of the stack, with compute trays above and
        below them.
      </p>
      <p>
        That placement is not aesthetic. Every compute tray has to reach every switch tray over
        copper, and copper's reach at NVLink signalling rates is short and expensive. Putting the
        switches in the middle halves the worst-case cable run. The whole vertical arrangement of
        the rack is a cable-length optimisation.<Cite ids={['sth-teardown', 'nv-ocp']} />
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        Group sizes above and below the switch band are not symmetric — public teardowns show
        eight trays in one group and ten in the other, with power shelves interleaved at the top,
        middle and bottom of the stack. The asymmetry follows from busbar tap placement and from
        the height the CDU occupies at the base, not from anything electrical about the fabric.
      </p>
      <p>
        Service implications are worth stating plainly. Every tray is front-serviceable and
        blind-mates both its data and its liquid connections, so the mean time to replace a
        compute tray is a matter of minutes and does not involve breaking the coolant loop. The
        failure domain, however, is not the tray: losing one tray removes four GPUs from a
        72-GPU NVLink domain, and the collective operations of any job spanning that domain must
        be reformed. The <T id="ras">RAS engine</T> exists to make that a scheduled event rather
        than a surprise.
      </p>
    </L>

    <h2 id="parts">Every part, in text</h2>

    <p>
      The same descriptions the model shows when you click a part. This list is the
      accessible path: nothing in the 3D view is stated only there.
    </p>

    <div class="part-list bleed">
      {partInfo.map((p) => (
        <div data-group={p.group} id={`part-${p.id}`}>
          <h3>{p.label} <span class="count">{p.count}</span></h3>
          <p><strong>{p.summary}</strong></p>
          <p>{p.detail}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <Callout kind="myth">
      <p>
        <strong>"It's just a bigger DGX."</strong> A DGX or HGX system is a server: a baseboard of
        eight GPUs, an x86 host, PCIe between them, and NVLink that stops at the chassis wall.
        NVL72 moves the unit of integration from the server to the rack — the host CPU changes
        architecture, PCIe leaves the critical path, and the enclosure itself becomes the thing
        that is designed. <a href="/comparison">The comparison chapter</a> works through what
        actually differs.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="numbers">Mechanical figures</h2>

    <SpecTable
      caption="Rack"
      ids={['rack-height', 'rack-width', 'rack-depth', 'rack-weight', 'compute-trays', 'switch-trays', 'switch-asics', 'superchips', 'rack-power']}
    />

    <Deeper title="Why 1.36 tonnes is a bigger problem than it sounds">
      <p>
        Spread over the rack's roughly 0.72 m² footprint, <S id="rack-weight" /> is well over a
        tonne per square metre — before you account for the fact that raised-floor tiles are rated
        for distributed load, not for four castors. Many existing halls are rated around 700–1,200
        kg/m², so an NVL72 is at or past the limit of the floor it would sit on, and the delivery
        path to get it there has its own limits.
      </p>
      <p>
        Combined with the <S id="retrofit-cost" /> typically quoted to add liquid cooling to
        existing space,<Cite ids="schneider" /> this is a large part of why these racks
        concentrate in new build rather than spreading into existing colocation.
      </p>
    </Deeper>

    <h2 id="spine">The spine deserves its own paragraph</h2>

    <p>
      At the back of the rack, four cable cartridges carry <S id="spine-cables" /> copper
      conductors between the compute trays and the switch trays. Jensen Huang's description —
      "5,000 NVLink cables, in total, 2 miles" — is close to NVIDIA's own OCP filing, which
      specifies four cartridges delivering <S id="allreduce" /> of AllReduce
      bandwidth.<Cite ids="nv-ocp" />
    </p>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        The decision to use copper rather than optics is the single most consequential engineering
        choice in the rack, and it was made on a power budget. NVIDIA's stated position is that
        the transceivers and retimers for an optical spine would have drawn about
        <S id="optical-penalty" />; SemiAnalysis arrived at ~19.4 kW independently by counting 648
        1.6T transceivers at roughly 30 W each.<Cite ids="semianalysis-optical" />
      </p>
      <p>
        Against a <S id="rack-power" /> budget, that is roughly one sixth of the rack's entire
        power draw spent on moving data a metre and a half. Copper at that reach costs almost
        nothing by comparison. The rack's shape — switches centred, trays close-packed, spine
        short — falls out of the requirement that copper remain viable.
      </p>
    </L>

    <Callout kind="caveat">
      <p>
        Cable count is quoted as "over 5,000" by NVIDIA and as 5,184 by several secondary
        sources. Both appear here as a range rather than a single number; see
        <a href="/sources#disputed">the disputed-figures list</a>.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </div>
</Base>
